tly  improves the reach of your content so  let's now deep dive on the hook so on  the first sentences that you leave on  your post here what you need to  understand that after a couple of lines  there is the so called fold so linkedin  only shows the beginning of the post and  after a couple of lines then it just  says see more and people need to click  on see more to see the remainder of the  post so here what's important is that  you're able to place the full hook  before the fold make sure the full hook  is seen before then this fold arrives  and the good thing is that where this  fold is placed is predictable and it  depends on whether there is an  attachment to the post or not and if you  follow the recommendation that i just  gave you a moment ago you will almost  always have such an attachment attached  to the post and if this is the case then  this fold appears after three lines of  content so you see this here in the left  example three lines and then the fold  appears and if there's no attachment  this fold appears after five lines so  here in the example to the right you see  four lines of text but then there's also  an empty line an empty line this counts  as way so after five lines then there  will be the fold so of course with your  hook you want to encourage people to  click on see more so maybe you are  ending with a question in the hook that  then you are answering in the remainder  of the post or maybe you make a rather  controversial statement then you explain  in the remainder of the post so  encourage people to click on see more  because of course just the fact alone  the people click on see more is in  itself already a positive sign to the  algorithm that people find this most  interesting and they want to learn more  about it let's not talk about another  little hack that you can do in your  content strategy to boost the reach of  your posts and this is occasionally  creating posts that really focus on  boosting engagement focus on getting as  much engagement as possible and one type  of post that is very suited to do that  are surveys see in this example you can  see that i created a survey it got about  40 000 impressions of 40 000 views and  there were about 2 000 people who  participated in the survey who clicked  on an option in the survey so two  thousand people is quite a lot for a  post of myself so you can often see that  service get quite a lot of engagement  like this and the good thing is if a  person interacted with your content in  this way even though this person did not  necessarily follow you then this person  is more likely to in the future see  posts that you created that you wrote  because linkedin now thinks that hey  this person might be interested in your  content so i would not encourage you to  always only create posts like this  because the truth is also only because  someone participated in the survey  doesn't necessarily mean that that  person is really interested in your  content doesn't necessarily mean that  now the person will go to your profile  and follow you but occasional posts like  this can help to increase your  engagement increase your views in the  medium term just one word of caution in  the past also earlier this year linkedin  was quite swamped with posts where  people were doing surveys not with the  survey 2 which i think is also rather  new on linkedin but rather by abusing  the different types of like buttons they  could participate in the server by  clicking the like button or the  insightful like button or the love like  button and so on and using that as a  survey and by that of course getting  lots of engagement so recently i've not  really seen any of these types of posts  anymore on my timeline so i think here  this is an example of how linkedin  adjusted the algorithm to not punish  these types of posts which is why at the  moment i would not recommend you to  create this type of content so let's not  talk about another strategy for your  content and this is the built-in public  strategy maybe you've already heard  about it there was quite a lot of buzz  around it and the main idea is this is  especially suitable for maybe  entrepreneurs so anyone in leadership  position that you just talk about what  you're building about the company the  organization that you're building and  just regularly share some insights on  that maybe once per week you share your  biggest learnings of the last week or  the biggest achievements what you learn  from them maybe also some failures what  you learn from that so this is just a  strategy where you can really lift the  curtains let people in into your  business and provide value down the road  this can of course help to create trust  with potential employees with potential  customers this is a great strategy to  create new leads i'm just showing you  here an example of a gentleman so for  full disclosure i don't know this  gentleman i also don't want to endorse  any of his content or his activities i  just thought this was a nice example of  where someone is doing exactly that  there are many other examples out there  so let's now talk about some typical  mistakes that many people are doing and  here i'm now referencing this book from  the great gary vaynerchuk jab jab jab  right hook maybe some of you have seen  it and the big notion of this book is  that if you want to be successful in  social media in the world out there  rather focus on the jabs and be rather  measured in your use of right hooks and  in this picture the jab is a piece of  content a post that focuses on  delivering value for the audience  whereas the right hook is the message  where you have some type of commercial  interest maybe you want someone to  become a customer of yourself and as the  title already says jab jab jab right  hook right so for every right hook for  every commercial message make sure that  there's a good number of really value  creating messages before that or to say  differently your profile should not look  like a big advertisement this is frankly  what i sometimes see people also do  wrong with this built-in public strategy  where they just talk about how great  their organizations are great their  company is this is not what people want  to read all the time in your content  focus on delivering value for others and  if you do that consistently then people  will very much understand if you  occasionally also place this right hook  with your own commercial interest let's  now talk about sharing other posts and  in principle there's nothing wrong with  that you just need to be aware that how  the system the algorithm works is that  if you share another post that mainly  helps the reach of the other posts so  the other posts will often be shown to  more people potentially also to more  people in your audience so the post that  you yourself create by sharing another  post potentially also with some comments  this post in itself will often only get  a fraction of the views than such a post  would usually get so sharing other posts  is great for the post that you're  sharing but often doesn't help you if  you want to create a post yourself that  gets lots of reach lots of views so  depending on your goals you might still  want to share the other post to support  it or you just decide to create a new  post by yourself that picks up some of  the main messages and then has higher  chances to get the reach let's now also  talk about linking external resources  for instance adding a link to another  website to your post and here the truth  is also that this is very bad for the  reach of your posts i've recently seen  an analysis of another person with a  conclusion the post with external links  receive on average only one sixth of the  reach of a post without external links  and the reason for that i think is also  quite obvious of course linkedin likes  to keep people on the platform linkedin  doesn't like to leave people from the  platform which is why they are punishing  posts that have these external links so  my advice would be that if you do want  to link to an external page don't put  the link in the original post but rather  put the link in the comments and then  you can write on the post something like  okay link to the page in the comments  and this will then to some extent bypass  this reach penalty that linkedin  otherwise would put on your post but now  i would love to hear from you do you  have any other linkedin strategies and  tips to share leave a comment here in  the comment section i'd love to get all  your insights and learn from you as well  again if you enjoyed it or took any  value from it please hit the like button  for the algorithm and also subscribe and  follow to stay up to date on all the  content big big thanks to the members of  from learning as well thank you for your  support my name is heinrich i'm the  creator from learning talk to you again  soon
 

